  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nThe upper air pattern early this morning shows a trough axis \nextending from Ontario through the Upper Midwest into KS, a ridge \npushing into the PNW, and a weaker low over SoCal and the Baja \nPeninsula of Mexico. At the surface, low pressure has moved into the \nGreat Lakes region with its cold front extending into the Lower MS \nRiver Valley, while high pressure dominates the western half of the \nCONUS. This puts us firmly within CAA as northwest winds remain \nbreezy. Winds will increase during the daytime hours today as mixing \ncommences, gusting 30-40mph at times while sustained at 15-25mph. \nStratus clouds can be seen upstream on satellite with some wrap-\naround low-level moisture behind the departing system, so between \nthat and the colder air mass, highs are only forecast to top out in \nthe low to mid 50s today.\n\nSkies should clear out mid to late afternoon and winds should \ndiminish by sunset as the pressure gradient relaxes and mixing \nsubsides. With the sfc high moving directly overhead tonight into \nSaturday morning, this sets the stage for a good radiational cooling \nscenario. NBM morning lows sit around the 25th percentile of all \nguidance, which seems reasonable for this setup. Looking at the \nrange of models, still think that the area for the going Freeze \nWatch pretty well captures where temperatures have at least a 50% \nchance of reaching or falling just below freezing. In collaborating \nwith neighboring offices, opted to leave the watch in place for now \nand issue the Frost Advisory elsewhere as those areas are likely to \nfall to the low to mid 30s.\n\nThe colder temperatures look short-lived with a warming trend taking \nshape the rest of the weekend into next week. The sfc high moves \neast Saturday afternoon with the upper ridge moving in as the low to \nour southwest stays cut off. This favors a return to southwest flow \nin the low levels and eventually aloft, helping temperatures to warm \nto the upper 50s Saturday and eventually the 60s and 70s early next \nweek. By Wednesday and Thursday, the upper pattern becomes more \namplified with a deepening trough on the Pacific coast and the ridge \nstrengthening its hold over the Southern and Central Plains. Some \nlow 80s return to the forecast by that point. Dry weather dominates \nmost of next week with low rain chances returning late Thursday into \nFriday as the western trough approaches.",
